From 7d9819cd765c2b46a9f9aabbc225f0bf1edde5bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:51:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] updates: a signed update channel of LibreELEC Hades, off
 until one exists

LibreELEC Hades does not use LibreELEC's update server (its builds would
replace Hades), so nothing could be looked for or downloaded by itself.
This is the side of the system for a channel of its own, made so that a
channel cannot be used to install anything that LibreELEC Hades did not
publish, nor an older version:

- the system takes part in a channel only when its image has one in
  /usr/share/hades/update: channel.conf (the HTTPS address of the manifest
  and the name of the channel), keys/*.pub (the Ed25519 keys that may sign
  it) and release-serial (the serial of this release). No image has one
  yet, so nothing changes: no address is asked;
- the manifest is JSON, and its signature a file next to it: Ed25519 (RFC
  8032) over a prefix that names the format and the manifest, checked with
  the pycryptodome of the image before the manifest is read. A key that
  the image does not have, a changed byte or a signature made for
  something else: nothing is taken;
- a manifest older than the newest taken from the channel is refused (one
  served again cannot hide a newer version), and so is an expired one,
  which needs the clock set over the network (connman or timesyncd say
  so): without it, nothing is taken;
- the release is offered only when its serial is higher than the image's,
  for this channel and this computer (DISTRO_ARCH);
- its file (an update .tar) comes over HTTPS only, with the certificates
  of the system (a redirect to anything else is refused), is read up to
  the size of the manifest, and is moved into the update folder only when
  its size and SHA-256 are the manifest's; one download at a time, and on
  any error nothing of it is kept.

It is used as LibreELEC's server was: the service looks every six hours
and, with "auto", downloads the version (installed at the next start),
with "manual" only tells; "Available Versions" asks, says what it found
and asks before downloading and restarting. With custom channels shown,
"Available Versions" is LibreELEC's, as before. The help of "Automatic
updates" with a channel says what is taken, in English and the three
Spanish of Hades; the messages for "the newest", "could not be checked"
and "not the signed file" too.

Tested inside the system of the published image (its Python, pycryptodome
and OpenSSL), with keys and a reference signature made on the build host
with python-cryptography and openssl pkeyutl, and an HTTPS server on
127.0.0.1: 131 checks, and each of 18 changes that take out one of these
protections makes the test fail. Not run in Kodi.
